Fillets: a vanishing end moved to its vertex on a periodic face keeps the
contact's p-curve in its period where the vertex lies on the face's seam
(mitcad#1374).

ChFi3d_ShrinkVanishingEnd (patch 0130) moves the end of a rounding that
vanishes at its spine's end vertex to that vertex, setting the last pole
of each contact's p-curve to the vertex's parameters on its face,
BRep_Tool::Parameters. On a periodic face whose seam passes through the
vertex, those are the parameters on one side of the seam, a period from
the other. A boss's foot rounded with 1 on a plate's top whose side's top
edge was rounded first: the foot's tangent chain runs on along the side's
blend's cut on the boss's cylinder to the vertex where that blend ends, on
the cylinder's seam. The walk ended there at u = pi/2, the contact on the
cylinder coming from u > pi/2, but the moved pole took the vertex's
u = 5 pi/2, from the seam's other side: the p-curve ran across the whole
period at its last span, and the cylinder's wire crossed itself (an
invalid result). The vertex's parameters are now taken in the period of
the contact's end, a whole number of periods from the face's own.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_boss_tangent.cpp, the plate's top edge
and the boss's foot rounded together with 1 (the convex one first, the
concave one on its result, as MitCad's fillet and OCCT's fillet one after
the other build them) on the synthetic bodies and on the case of
MITCAD_GEOMETRY_CASES: valid, the volume the one after the other gives;
36 checks fail without the patch.

Upstream: new.

